      "id": "okf-format-assessment-against-ard-substrate",
      "title": "OKF v0.1 versus the research substrate — adoption assessment",
      "summary": "OKF should be treated as an interchange boundary rather than the research substrate because its permissive format does not preserve source-bound attestation and verification invariants.",
      "updated": "2026-07-19",
      "status": null,
      "relationships": []
    },
    {
      "path": ".research/briefs/priority-and-grooming-shapes-for-agentic-substrates.md",
      "namespace": "research",
      "kind": "research-brief",
      "id": "priority-and-grooming-shapes-for-agentic-substrates",
      "title": "Priority and grooming, translated for an agent-drained work substrate",
      "summary": "For an agent-drained work substrate, priority should be a structural ordering signal (value field optional and conditional) and grooming should be a propose-not-prune capability mechanizing only what date/metadata supports.",
      "updated": "2026-06-15",
      "status": null,
      "relationships": []
    },
    {
      "path": ".research/briefs/priority-signaling-mechanisms-landscape.md",
      "namespace": "research",
      "kind": "research-brief",
      "id": "priority-signaling-mechanisms-landscape",
      "title": "Priority-Signaling Mechanisms in Agile and Lean Systems",
      "summary": "Common agile/lean priority mechanisms split into machine-sortable forms (computed numeric scores like WSJF/CD3 and RICE; ranked 1-N position) and human-ceremony artifacts (MoSCoW, Kano, value/effort quadrants, P1/P2/P3 flags); categorical flags inflate to uselessness without an enforced scarcity constraint.",
      "updated": "2026-06-15",
      "status": null,
      "relationships": []
    },
